  "question": {
    "text": "The lines $p\\left(p^2+1\\right) x-y+q=0$ and $\\left(p^2+1\\right)^2 x+\\left(p^2+1\\right) y+2 q=0$ are perpendicular to a common line for"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\nno value of $p$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\nexactly one value of $p$\n",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "\nexactly two values of $p$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\nmore than two values of $p$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Lines must be parallel, therefore slopes are equal $\\Rightarrow p\\left(p^2+1\\right)=-\\left(p^2+1\\right) \\Rightarrow p=-1$"
  }
}